---
import PublicImage from '../components/PublicImage.astro';
import PageJumpLinks from '../components/PageJumpLinks.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import HeroSection from '../components/HeroSection.astro';
import BookingButton from '../components/BookingButton.astro';
import FAQSection from '../components/FAQSection.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Prolotherapy For Arthritis"
  description="Prolotherapy is a regenerative injection treatment that helps relieve arthritis pain, improve range of motion and promote tissue repair. Learn about prolotherapy with Dr. Colin MacLeod, ND in Halifax."
  focusKeyword="prolotherapy arthritis Halifax"
>
  <HeroSection
    title="Prolotherapy For Arthritis"
    compact
    video={{ src: "https://www.youtube.com/embed/SBGjz31fZVI", title: "Prolotherapy Overview" }}
  >
    <nav slot="breadcrumb" class="text-emerald-300 text-sm">
      <a href="/prolotherapy/" class="hover:text-white transition-colors">Prolotherapy</a>
      <span class="mx-2">/</span>
      <span class="text-white">Arthritis</span>
    </nav>
    <p>Prolotherapy has the strongest evidence base for osteoarthritis treatment, with 16 clinical studies showing significant improvements in pain, stiffness and range of motion.</p>
  </HeroSection>

  <PageJumpLinks items={[{"id": "pain-relief", "title": "Research overview"}, {"id": "prolotherapy", "title": "Getting started"}, {"id": "joints-treated", "title": "Joints treated"}, {"id": "how-prolotherapy-works", "title": "How it works"}, {"id": "growth-factors-promote-repair", "title": "Tissue repair"}, {"id": "how-long-do-results-last", "title": "Duration of results"}, {"id": "prolotherapy-vs-hyaluronic-acid--cortisone", "title": "Other treatments"}]} />

  <!-- Three Benefits Section -->
  <section id="pain-relief" class="care-section">
    <div class="section-shell">
      <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6 lg:gap-8">
        <!-- Pain Relief -->
        <div class="care-item">

          <h2 class="text-xl font-serif font-medium mb-3 text-emerald-950">Pain Relief</h2>
          <p class="text-slate-600">Sixteen studies have investigated prolotherapy for osteoarthritis. Fifteen concluded that prolotherapy significantly reduces pain, stiffness and improves range of motion.<sup>1-16</sup></p>
        </div>

        <!-- High Success Rate - Original WordPress SVG -->
        <div class="care-item">

          <h2 class="text-xl font-serif font-medium mb-3 text-emerald-950">High Success Rate</h2>
          <p class="text-slate-600">Research has shown success rates at or near 80%.<sup>1,9</sup> We have seen success rates of 80-85% in our clinic for patients with osteoarthritis.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Lasting Results - Original WordPress Calendar SVG -->
        <div class="care-item">

          <h2 class="text-xl font-serif font-medium mb-3 text-emerald-950">Lasting Results</h2>
          <p class="text-slate-600">In the longest study on prolotherapy (2.5 years), improvement in pain progressively increased throughout the entire study period.<sup>9</sup></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Pricing Section -->
  <section id="prolotherapy" class="care-section">
    <div class="section-shell">
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-12">
        <!-- Prolotherapy Card -->
        <div class="care-item">
          <div class="text-left mb-6">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-serif font-medium text-emerald-950 mb-4">Prolotherapy</h2>
            <!-- Syringe Icon - Original WordPress SVG -->

            <p class="text-slate-600">Regenerative injection treatment</p>
          </div>

          <ul class="space-y-3 mb-8">
            <li class="flex items-center">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-600 mr-3 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"></path>
              </svg>
              <span>Pain relief</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-center">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-600 mr-3 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"></path>
              </svg>
              <span>Improved range of motion</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-center">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-600 mr-3 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"></path>
              </svg>
              <span>Improved stiffness</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-center">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-600 mr-3 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"></path>
              </svg>
              <span>Tissue repair</span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- Getting Started Card -->
        <div class="care-item">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-serif font-medium text-emerald-950 mb-6">Getting Started with Prolotherapy</h2>

          <p class="text-slate-600 mb-8">
            An initial consultation is required to determine whether prolotherapy is appropriate, discuss alternatives and review potential benefits, uncertainty, risks and costs. An assessment does not guarantee that treatment will be offered or effective.
          </p>

          <BookingButton variant="primary" surface="light" size="lg" fullWidth source="prolotherapy-for-arthritis-pricing" placement="inline" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Joints We Treat Section -->
  <section id="joints-treated" class="care-section">
    <div class="section-shell">
      <div class="text-left mb-8">
        <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-serif font-medium text-emerald-950 mb-4">Joints We Treat</h2>
        <p class="text-lg text-slate-600 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Prolotherapy is effective for osteoarthritis in many joints
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-12 items-center">
        <div class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-xl">
          <PublicImage
            src="/images/treatments/knee-examination.webp"
            alt="Dr. MacLeod examining a patient's knee for osteoarthritis assessment"
            class="w-full h-full object-cover"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>

        <div>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-8">
            {['Knee', 'Hip', 'Thumb (CMC joint)', 'Fingers (DIP/PIP)', 'Shoulder', 'Ankle', 'Wrist', 'Elbow'].map(joint => (
              <div class="flex items-center">
                <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-600 mr-3 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                  <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"></path>
                </svg>
                <span class="text-slate-700">{joint}</span>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>

          <p class="text-slate-600 mb-6">
            The strongest evidence exists for knee osteoarthritis, with 16 clinical studies demonstrating effectiveness. Studies have also shown benefit for thumb and finger joints, with Reeves et al. demonstrating significant improvement in hand OA patients.
          </p>

          <a
            href="#how-prolotherapy-works"
            class="text-emerald-700 font-semibold hover:text-emerald-800 transition-colors inline-flex items-center"
          >
            Learn how prolotherapy works for arthritis
            <svg class="w-5 h-5 ml-2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path>
            </svg>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- How Does Prolotherapy Work Section -->
  <section id="how-prolotherapy-works" class="care-section care-section--tint">
    <div class="section-shell">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-serif font-medium text-emerald-950 mb-6">How Does Prolotherapy Work?</h2>
        <div class="prose prose-lg prose-invert">
          <p class="text-slate-600">
            When the concentrated dextrose solution used in prolotherapy is injected, the local cells respond by releasing a burst of growth factors.<sup>27-30</sup> This response from our cells is rapid, with growth factor production rising within minutes to hours of cells being exposed to a concentrated dextrose solution.<sup>31</sup>
          </p>
          <p class="text-slate-600">
            This surge in local growth factor production is believed to be the cause of the improvement seen in osteoarthritic joints, as well as in other painful, degenerative conditions.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Growth Factors Section -->
  <section id="growth-factors-promote-repair" class="care-section">
    <div class="section-shell">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-serif font-medium text-emerald-950 mb-6">Growth Factors Promote Repair</h2>
        <div class="prose prose-lg text-slate-600">
          <p>
            The main "repair factors" naturally present within joint cartilage, bone and synovial fluid are insulin-like growth factor (IGF), transforming growth factor beta (TGF-β), epidermal growth factor (EGF), basic fibroblast growth factor (bFGF) and platelet derived growth factor (PDGF). These growth factors encourage the repair process within the joint and surrounding bone.<sup>32,33</sup>
          </p>
          <p>
            Conversely, interleukin-1 (IL-1) and tumor necrosis factor (TNF) are considered "disrepair factors" within the joint cartilage, bone and synovial fluid. These disrepair factors lead to the breakdown of tissue and limit the effects of the repairing growth factors.<sup>34-38</sup> Research has shown that dextrose has a down-regulating effect on IL-1 and collagenase.<sup>39</sup>
          </p>
          <p>
            Animal and in vitro studies have investigated the effects of growth factors on the health of joint cartilage and bone. These studies have found that elevated levels of growth factors within a joint led to chondrogenesis (formation of cartilage cells)<sup>40</sup>, thickening of cartilage<sup>41</sup> and even repair of full thickness cartilage defects.<sup>42</sup>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- How Long Do Results Last Section -->
  <section id="how-long-do-results-last" class="care-section">
    <div class="section-shell">
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-12 items-center">
        <div>
          <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-serif font-medium text-emerald-950 mb-6">How Long Do Results Last?</h2>
          <div class="prose prose-lg text-slate-600">
            <p>
              The improvements achieved with prolotherapy are typically long lasting. In the longest of all of the studies on prolotherapy, which lasted 2.5 years, the improvement in pain progressively improved up until the end of the study.
            </p>
            <p>
              The authors of this study stated that "prolotherapy resulted in safe, significant, progressive improvement of knee pain, function and stiffness scores among most participants through a mean follow-up of 2.5 years and may be an appropriate therapy for patients with knee osteoarthritis refractory to other conservative care."<sup>9</sup>
            </p>
            <p>
              In this study improvement increased most rapidly within 10 weeks of the first treatment. Following this initial improvement, a continual progressive improvement was seen up until the end of the study, 2.5 years later.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-xl">
          <PublicImage
            src="/images/charts/prolotherapy-osteoarthritis-graph.webp"
            alt="Prolotherapy treatment results graph showing progressive improvement over 2.5 years"
            class="w-full h-full object-cover"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Prolotherapy vs Other Treatments Section -->
  <section id="prolotherapy-vs-hyaluronic-acid--cortisone" class="care-section">
    <div class="section-shell">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-serif font-medium text-emerald-950 mb-6">Prolotherapy Vs. Hyaluronic Acid & Cortisone</h2>
        <div class="prose prose-lg text-slate-600">
          <p>
            Hyaluronic acid is another injectable treatment which can be used into an arthritic joint to help treat the associated pain and disability. Hyaluronic acid is a natural component of our skin, eyes and joints. It is an important component of healthy and strong cartilage. For these reasons, hyaluronic acid has been studied and is used clinically as an intra-articular injection for osteoarthritis.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three studies have compared the effectiveness of prolotherapy and hyaluronic acid injections for osteoarthritic joints. All three of these studies found that prolotherapy was a superior treatment for the management of pain and disability associated with an arthritic joint.<sup>12-14</sup>
          </p>
          <p>
            One study has investigated the effectiveness of prolotherapy compared to an intra-articular injection of a corticosteroid (cortisone) and found both treatments to be effective in the short term but that prolotherapy yielded superior results over the long term (6 months).<sup>8</sup>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Safety Section -->
  <section id="safety-of-prolotherapy" class="care-section">
    <div class="section-shell">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-serif font-medium text-emerald-950 mb-6">Safety of Prolotherapy</h2>
        <div class="prose prose-lg text-slate-600">
          <p class="text-slate-600">
            Prolotherapy is an invasive procedure. Potential effects include pain, stiffness, bruising, swelling, bleeding, infection, reaction to an ingredient and anatomical-area-specific complications. The likelihood and significance of these risks vary by procedure and individual health.
          </p>
          <p class="text-slate-600">
            As with any injection there is a small chance of infection. There is also a small chance of allergic reaction to the local anesthetic (numbing agent) which is used at the site of the injection.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FAQSection bg="white" sectionClass="bg-white pt-20 lg:pt-24 pb-12 lg:pb-16" items={[
          { question: "Does Prolotherapy Hurt?", answer: "<p>There can be some discomfort associated with prolotherapy but it is typically short-lived. The needle size used is smaller than a needle for drawing blood or a cortisone injection, and a local anesthetic is included in the solution. After treatment, it is normal to experience temporary soreness, stiffness or mild swelling at the injection site for 1-4 days as part of the healing response. Bruising is possible. Infection is a rare risk with any injection procedure. Serious complications are rare and have not been reported in published clinical trials of dextrose prolotherapy.</p>" },
          { question: "How Many Injections Will I Need?", answer: "<p>Six treatment sessions at a rate of one injection every 1-2 weeks yields optimal results for osteoarthritis. If there is pain in the region of ligaments or tendons surrounding the joint these can also be treated effectively with prolotherapy.</p>" },
          { question: "What Do I Do After Prolotherapy?", answer: "<p>After a prolotherapy treatment it is best to limit exercise to less intense activities for 1-2 days to allow the treated region to complete the repair process unhindered.</p>" },
          { question: "Who Should Not Have Prolotherapy?", answer: "<p>Prolotherapy is not appropriate if you are pregnant, have had a joint replacement at the affected site, have post-infectious arthritis, active infection or are on blood thinners. A full health history is reviewed before treatment to confirm suitability.</p>" },
          { question: "Is Prolotherapy Ok For Diabetics?", answer: "<p>Yes. The amount of sugar present in a prolotherapy injection, 1-2 grams, is very small compared to the amount in many sweet foods. For example, an apple has 19g, a Snickers bar has 22g and a 12-oz glass of orange juice has 33g of sugar.</p>" },
        ]} />

